---
layout: default
title: [[ page.title ]]
---
<style>
  ul.qlist { list-style:none; margin:1.6rem 0 0; padding:0; }
  ul.qlist li { border-top:1px solid var(--hair); padding:1rem 0; }
  ul.qlist a.title { font-size:1.08rem; font-weight:600; }
  ul.qlist p { margin:.35rem 0 .3rem; font-size:.92rem; }
  .meta { font-size:.78rem; color:var(--muted); }
  .tag { display:inline-block; border:1px solid var(--hair);
         border-radius:3px; padding:.05rem .4rem; margin-right:.4rem;
         font-size:.72rem; color:var(--muted); background:var(--band); }
</style>

<p>[[ page.intro ]]</p>

<p class="meta">
  Every query below runs in your browser: the graph is fetched as a static
  Turtle file and queried with rdflib under Pyodide. There is no endpoint,
  nothing you type leaves your machine, and an archived copy of this
  repository stays queryable with no service to keep alive. The first page
  you open downloads roughly 10&nbsp;MB of Python runtime; after that the
  browser has it cached.
</p>

<ul class="qlist">
[% for q in queries %]  <li>
    <a class="title" href="[[ q.id ]].html">[[ q.title ]]</a>
    <p>[[ q.blurb ]]</p>
    <p class="meta">
      <span class="tag">[[ q.view_label ]]</span>
      <span class="tag">[[ q.rows_at_build ]] rows when built</span>
      <a href="rq/[[ q.id ]].rq">[[ q.id ]].rq</a>
    </p>
  </li>
[% endfor %][% for e in extras %]  <li>
    <a class="title" href="[[ e.href ]]">[[ e.title ]]</a>
    <p>[[ e.blurb ]]</p>
    <p class="meta"><span class="tag">[[ e.tag ]]</span></p>
  </li>
[% endfor %]</ul>

<p class="meta" style="margin-top:1.8rem">
  All of them on one page: <a href="all.html">all.html</a>. The plain
  <code>.rq</code> files are in <a href="rq/">rq/</a>, and the graph itself
  is <a href="[[ graph.page_url ]]">[[ graph.url ]]</a>
  ([[ graph.megabytes ]]&nbsp;MB).
</p>
